On the 29th, President Lee Jae-myung addressed the landmine explosion that occurred in the Demilitarized Zone (DMZ) in Paju, Gyeonggi Province, stating, “I offer my deepest sympathies to the injured soldiers and their families and wish them a speedy recovery.”

Presiding over a Cabinet meeting at the Blue House that day, President Lee noted, “Three soldiers sustained serious injuries in a landmine accident near the Military Demarcation Line last week.”

President Lee stated, “Military authorities must transparently uncover the truth of the incident through a swift and thorough investigation in close cooperation with the UN Command,” adding, “We will take necessary measures based on those findings.” Although military authorities had announced the previous day that there was a high probability the explosion was caused by North Korea, President Lee did not mention North Korea during his remarks.

President Lee remarked, “There is no distinction between ruling and opposition parties when it comes to national security,” and added, “I hope there will be no actions that jeopardize national security by fueling baseless conspiracy theories regarding this matter.”

These remarks by President Lee came eight days after he first expressed his wishes for the soldiers' recovery immediately following the accident on the 21st.

Prior to addressing the landmine accident, President Lee issued a message emphasizing ‘independent national defense.’ He stated, "A strong national defense is a key pillar for protecting national interests," adding, "We must accelerate the construction of nuclear-powered submarines—a crucial element of self-reliant defense—and the advancement of missile technologies, such as hypersonic missiles."

He continued, "We must persist in developing and deploying cutting-edge weapon systems to provide ironclad defense across the air, land, sea, and space using our own indigenous technology," and emphasized, "We must steadfastly strive to create a genuine peace where there is no need to fight, going beyond merely winning a conflict."

The President also noted, "The KF-21, the first fighter jet built with our own technology, was officially delivered to the Air Force last week." He added, "Including us, only eight nations worldwide have succeeded in independently developing a 4.5-generation fighter jet—a culmination of South Korea's advanced technology. This holds special significance for our self-reliant defense capabilities."

